 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: FRUS shows American officials from the 1950s to the late 1970s judging their standing in Asia by press comment, opinion surveys and the state of each alliance, and finding it fragile whenever policy was unpopular. Their consistent method was to speak through Asians and keep the American hand low: work \"through indigenous leadership\" (1952–1954, Volume XII, Part 2, Document 403), let Vietnamese persuade Vietnamese (1917–1972, Volume VII, Document 32), and keep \"US visibility\" low (1917–1972, Volume VIII, Document 31). They doubted information alone could move opinion. The ambassador in Tokyo judged Japan's response to the bombing of North Vietnam \"probably unchangeable over the short run\" (1964–1968, Volume XXIX, Part 2, Document 46). After Saigon fell, posts reported that programs on American security commitments \"lack credibility\" (1969–1976, Volume XXXVIII, Part 2, Document 105). The beliefs behind this were of an audience sensitive to race, war memory and Western dominance. The answer rests on fifteen documents.\n\nWhere FRUS points for the rest:\nSeries the printed documents came from (came-from channel; about three in five of the 119 regionally focused documents in the public diplomacy volumes came from the information agency's own records):\n- Subject Files of the Director, U.S. Information Agency, Office of the Director, Executive Secretariat — Record Group 306; FRUS cites it as \"Director's Subject Files,\" Entry A1–42 (1968–1972) and Entry UD WW 257 (1963–1969); NARA identifier 40967285; 1956–1972; partly restricted. A likely match by title and creator only: the transfer number FRUS gives is listed under eighteen series and not under this one.\n- Subject Files, U.S. Information Agency, Office of Policy and Plans — Record Group 306; NARA identifier 1257679; 1953–1971; partly restricted (also a likely match only, by transfer number).\n- Operations Coordinating Board Files, Lot 62 D 430 — Record Group 59, Executive Secretariat; NARA identifier 2839191; 1953–1961; partly restricted. The source of the board's country plans for East Asia.\n- Central Decimal File, classes for Asia's internal and national defense affairs and for Philippine international relations (the printed documents date from 1953 and 1954) — Record Group 59; no identifier in the reference files I used.\n- Robert F. Kennedy Papers, Attorney General Papers: General Correspondence — Kennedy Library; NARA identifier 193936; 1961–1964.\n- Papers of Leonard H. Marks, Work Files — Johnson Library; NARA identifier 50827304; 1965–1968.\n\nSeries the editors cited without printing (pointed-at channel; only about one in eleven of the same 119 documents carries such a pointer):\n- Papers of Leonard H. Marks — Johnson Library; NARA identifier 50809179; 1965–1968.\n- Records of the Deputy Secretary of State (Warren Christopher), Lot 81D113, Entry P–14 — Record Group 59; NARA identifier 1487627; 1975–1981; partly restricted.\n- Kennedy National Security Files; Nixon White House Central Files, Kissinger Office Files and National Security Council Files; Ford National Security Adviser files — presidential libraries; not resolved to identifiers in the reference files I used.\n\nWhat this request cannot get from FRUS: No document uses \"soft power\"; I searched every one of the 553 volumes for the phrase and found none, while in the same search a control phrase was found in every one of the 109 volumes I scoped and an impossible one in none, so I searched instead in the officials' own terms (\"information program,\" \"psychological strategy,\" the agency's name, \"image of the United States\"). FRUS prints the agency's own survey results only as summaries in memoranda; nearly two in three of the documents naming the agency in my scope sit in the five Public Diplomacy volumes, which select for policy, not for evidence of reception. The surveys themselves are on the shelf: the agency's Vietnamese Public Opinion Surveys (Record Group 306, NARA identifier 1127750) and its International Survey Research Reports (identifier 721251). Audience views in Asian languages, and anything after 1980, are beyond these volumes.",
